{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3472","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3472","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3472\",\"parent_revision\":\"17148116f84ce41602fd311d9a04fe654dd212868cd0535728023dd9d274e227\",\"root_revision\":\"17148116f84ce41602fd311d9a04fe654dd212868cd0535728023dd9d274e227\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: \\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: closed\\nadded: \\n-->\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- Default session listing returns both Claude and Codex sessions with an explicit provider.\\n- Direct paths auto-detect both formats.\\n- Stage 1 emits only human user turns; Codex project instructions, developer messages, reasoning, tool traffic, and duplicate response items never enter batches.\\n- A flagged Codex `line_index` resolves to the correct user turn and preceding assistant-visible text.\\n- Legacy and current Codex rollout fixtures pass.\\n- Existing Claude extraction/render tests remain green.\\n- Installed Codex plugin invokes its own MCP tools and produces a real PNG from a synthetic Codex rollout.\\n- No Codex SQLite schema dependency and no new agent packaging format are introduced.\\n- `.mcp.json` keeps the tested top-level `mcpServers` wrapper and relative `cwd: \\\".\\\"` launcher.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Problem\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nThe plugin is packaged for Codex and its MCP launcher is covered, but the mining implementation still assumes Claude Code records under `~/.claude/projects/`. Codex stores metadata in `$CODEX_HOME/state_*.sqlite` and transcripts in rollout JSONL under `$CODEX_HOME/sessions/**` plus `archived_sessions/`; its records are not Claude's `{type,message,toolUseResult}` shape.\\n\\nThe current `rtfp` skill also names Claude plugin agents directly, although portable Codex plugins package skills and MCP servers rather than Claude `agents/*.md` as first-class plugin entries.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Design Decision\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nCreate one deep session-reader seam inside the frustration-analyzer MCP implementation. Provider-specific parsing stays behind that seam; every existing tool consumes the same normalized models.\\n\\nDo not read Codex's SQLite database for the first slice. RTFP needs one transcript at a time, and rollout JSONL contains the session id, cwd, timestamps, user turns, and assistant-visible text. Avoiding SQLite removes an unstable schema dependency and still covers active, archived, parent, and subagent rollout files.\\n\\n### Normalized models\\n\\nUse Pydantic models for:\\n\\n- `SessionMessage`: `source_line`, `role` (`user|assistant`), `text`, `timestamp`\\n- `SessionSummary`: existing fields `file`, `project`, `modified`, `size_bytes`, `title`, plus `provider` and `session_id`\\n\\nKeep the external batch locator as `{file,line_index,text,token_count}`; `line_index` remains the raw 0-based JSONL line so flags can be resolved without a sidecar index.\\n\\n### Provider adapters\\n\\nClaude adapter:\\n- preserve current `type=user|assistant`, `message.content`, and `toolUseResult` behavior\\n- preserve the existing human-text filter\\n\\nCodex adapter:\\n- legacy rollouts: consume only `event_msg` records whose payload type is `user_message` or `agent_message`\\n- current rollouts: consume only `event_msg.item_completed` records whose item type is `UserMessage` or `AgentMessage`\\n- concatenate text blocks case-insensitively from item content\\n- ignore `response_item`, reasoning, tool calls/results, developer instructions, `turn_context`, token usage, and world state to prevent duplicate turns and false positives from injected AGENTS/system text\\n\\nAuto-detect the provider from record structure. Raise a precise unsupported-format error when neither adapter recognizes the file.\\n\\n### Discovery\\n\\nWith no explicit root, `list_sessions` scans:\\n\\n- Claude: `~/.claude/projects/**/*.jsonl`\\n- Codex: `${CODEX_HOME:-~/.codex}/sessions/**/rollout-*.jsonl`\\n- Codex archived: `${CODEX_HOME:-~/.codex}/archived_sessions/rollout-*.jsonl`\\n\\nAn explicit root remains supported for fixtures and direct use. Sort the merged result by mtime and add `provider` so the selection UI can label each session.\\n\\n### Tool routing\\n\\nRoute every transcript-reading path through the normalized reader:\\n\\n- `list_sessions`\\n- `extract_user_messages`\\n- `scan_transcripts`\\n- `get_context_window`\\n- `get_scenario`\\n- `generate_social_post`\\n\\n`get_context_window` selects the target by raw `source_line`, then returns the previous/next normalized conversational messages. Rendering is unchanged.\\n\\n### Skill portability\\n\\nKeep the existing Claude named agents. Update `skills/rtfp/SKILL.md` so:\\n\\n- it labels sessions by provider\\n- it accepts either provider without branching downstream artifact shapes\\n- Claude may use the existing named detector/reconstructor agents\\n- Codex delegates generic subagents with the already-specified detection and reconstruction contracts; for a single small batch the parent may perform the same contract directly\\n- it never treats manual source-repo reads as a substitute for missing plugin MCP tools\\n</div>\\n\\n## Implementation Plan\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n1. Add the normalized reader and provider detection, using stdlib JSONL parsing rather than adding another storage dependency.\\n2. Replace the Claude-specific DuckDB queries in the six transcript-reading paths with the normalized reader. Keep batching and rendering behavior unchanged; remove DuckDB only if no remaining production caller needs it.\\n3. Add colocated fixtures/tests for Claude, legacy Codex, and current Codex records. Include injected `response_item` user/developer content and duplicate assistant records to prove they are ignored.\\n4. Update the portable skill workflow and README. Leave Claude agent files in place unless their behavior must change.\\n5. Run targeted pytest and `prek` on changed files.\\n6. Validate the FastMCP protocol from outside the plugin directory.\\n7. Install a copied plugin through a disposable local marketplace and run `frustration-analyzer:rtfp` in a fresh Codex session against a synthetic rollout. Retain skill-injection evidence, native MCP calls, the three verbatim artifact fields, and the rendered PNG.\\n8. Run the equivalent Claude integration check when authentication is available; report it blocked rather than infer compatibility if unavailable.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Non Goals\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- Persistent corpus indexing or analytics dashboards\\n- Mining hidden reasoning or tool outputs\\n- Joining Codex parent/subagent threads into one conversation\\n- Depending on private Codex SQLite tables for metadata\\n- Mechanical redaction of transcript content\\n</div>\\n\\n## Evidence\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- Live Codex rollouts on 2026-09-10 show both supported presentation-event variants and show that `response_item` user records can contain injected AGENTS/environment context.\\n- Existing repo research classifies frustration-analyzer as an MCP-dependent deeper port and requires a tiny-transcript-to-PNG isolated validation.\\n- Official OpenAI docs describe portable plugins as skills plus MCP and document custom Codex agents separately under `.codex/agents/*.toml`.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Approved Scope Extension Time Window Session Sets\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nUsers may request a set such as \\\"all sessions that progressed during this week.\\\" Keep parsing and context retrieval per-session, but make discovery filterable and let the skill orchestrate the selected set.\\n\\n- Extend `list_sessions` with optional inclusive `modified_after`, exclusive `modified_before`, `provider` (`all|claude|codex`), and bounded `limit`.\\n- The calling agent resolves natural-language periods into explicit timezone-aware ISO 8601 bounds before calling MCP.\\n- \\\"Progressed\\\" means the session file's last modification time falls within the requested window for this first slice; expose the returned `modified` value so the interpretation is visible.\\n- When the user explicitly requests all matches, the skill extracts each matched session, fans detection out over the resulting batches, merges flags across files, and reconstructs the strongest overall incident plus optional runner-ups.\\n- Preserve `{file,line_index}` on every flag so reconstruction remains local to the originating transcript.\\n- Report the match count and provider split before analysis. Apply the explicit limit and report truncation rather than silently dropping sessions.\\n- Do not add a persistent corpus database or join parent/subagent transcripts.\\n\\nAcceptance additions:\\n- A timezone-aware weekly range returns only sessions whose modification time is in `[modified_after, modified_before)`.\\n- Provider filtering and limit/truncation metadata are tested.\\n- Cross-session flag merging preserves each source file and raw line index.\\n- Existing single-session direct-path behavior remains unchanged.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"d8e28e4de7e9ab448513a835622080c085d7eabd282a0fa12d5f291a61c3066e\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABUI3L2Q\"}"}